1
00:00:00,383 --> 00:00:05,123
The evening of the
twenty-eighth of July, 1402.

2
00:00:05,203 --> 00:00:08,363
A man in chains is led into a vast tent.

3
00:00:08,443 --> 00:00:12,517
Hours ago, he was master of
an empire stretching from the

4
00:00:12,567 --> 00:00:16,763
Danube to the Euphrates. They
called him: the Thunderbolt.

5
00:00:16,843 --> 00:00:21,833
Before him sits a lame old man who
has never lost a battle in his life…

6
00:00:21,913 --> 00:00:23,500
So how does a thunderbolt fall?

7
00:00:24,967 --> 00:00:31,967
THE THUNDERBOLT AND THE LAME
Bayezid I and Timur — the road to Ankara

8
00:00:34,683 --> 00:00:38,790
Thirteen years earlier,
on the plain of Kosovo,

9
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:43,173
Sultan Murad I was killed
in the hour of victory.

10
00:00:43,253 --> 00:00:47,087
On that same field his son
Bayezid took the throne,

11
00:00:47,137 --> 00:00:52,923
and ordered the death of his brother
Yakub, so that no one would contest it.

12
00:00:53,003 --> 00:00:58,803
He was twenty-nine, fierce
in battle, quick to anger…

13
00:00:58,883 --> 00:01:00,733
a man who could not wait.

14
00:01:01,550 --> 00:01:11,024
Within a few years he swept over the Turkmen
emirates of Anatolia one after another:

15
00:01:11,074 --> 00:01:16,970
Aydın, Saruhan, Menteşe,
Germiyan, and then Karaman.

16
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:21,316
He appeared with his army
where no one expected him,

17
00:01:21,366 --> 00:01:26,180
crossing from Europe to Asia
as if distance meant nothing.

18
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:31,230
So the people named him
Yıldırım: the Thunderbolt.

19
00:01:31,310 --> 00:01:34,870
But the deposed princes did not forget…

20
00:01:34,950 --> 00:01:37,033
and they carried their vengeance east.

21
00:01:37,883 --> 00:01:39,603
Then Europe came for him.

22
00:01:39,683 --> 00:01:47,147
In 1396 the knights of Hungary, France
and Germany marched in a great crusade;

23
00:01:47,197 --> 00:01:54,133
he met them at Nicopolis on the Danube
and crushed them in a single day.

24
00:01:54,213 --> 00:01:57,977
At his side fought Stefan
Lazarević, prince of the Serbs,

25
00:01:58,027 --> 00:02:01,321
son of the prince killed
at Kosovo, who had become

26
00:02:01,371 --> 00:02:04,783
Bayezid's brother-in-law
and his most loyal vassal.

27
00:02:04,863 --> 00:02:09,300
We will meet him again, in
the Sultan's darkest hour.

28
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:17,259
Bayezid returned to Bursa crowned with
glory, laid siege to Constantinople,

29
00:02:17,309 --> 00:02:23,800
and asked the Abbasid Caliph in
Cairo for the title 'Sultan of Rum'.

30
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:25,960
It seemed to him that no power on
earth could stand against him.

31
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:28,790
And that is exactly where the danger begins…

32
00:02:28,870 --> 00:02:35,467
when a man believes the world
has grown smaller than he is.

33
00:02:36,317 --> 00:02:40,717
But in the east there was a man
who walked on only one sound leg.

34
00:02:40,797 --> 00:02:45,927
Timur was born near the city
of Kesh, in Transoxiana.

35
00:02:46,007 --> 00:02:50,672
In his youth arrows struck
his right leg and right hand,

36
00:02:50,722 --> 00:02:54,007
and he limped for the rest of his life.

37
00:02:54,087 --> 00:03:00,567
His enemies mocked him as
Timur-i Lang: Timur the Lame.

38
00:03:00,647 --> 00:03:02,900
None of them laughed for long.

39
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:08,275
In thirty years he forged the
tribes of Transoxiana into

40
00:03:08,325 --> 00:03:12,900
a war machine the world had
not seen since Genghis Khan.

41
00:03:12,980 --> 00:03:19,483
He overran Persia and Iraq, crushed the
Golden Horde on the northern steppes,

42
00:03:19,533 --> 00:03:24,640
and entered Delhi, returning
with plunder and war elephants.

43
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:33,933
Cities that resisted him became ruins, with
towers of skulls rising at their gates.

44
00:03:34,783 --> 00:03:41,589
He is credited with saying: just
as there is one God in heaven,

45
00:03:41,639 --> 00:03:45,883
there should be only one king on earth.

46
00:03:45,963 --> 00:03:48,685
By the end of the century,
the borders of the

47
00:03:48,735 --> 00:03:52,183
Thunderbolt and of the Lame
had met in eastern Anatolia.

48
00:03:52,263 --> 00:03:55,533
One land…

49
00:03:55,613 --> 00:04:01,467
and two kings, neither of
whom knew how to retreat.

50
00:04:02,317 --> 00:04:06,097
The two courts became rival refuges.

51
00:04:06,177 --> 00:04:13,267
To Timur fled the Anatolian princes
Bayezid had deposed, urging him to strike.

52
00:04:13,347 --> 00:04:19,108
To Bayezid fled Timur's enemies:
Sultan Ahmad Jalayir, lord

53
00:04:19,158 --> 00:04:25,167
of Baghdad, and Qara Yusuf,
chief of the Black Sheep Turkmen.

54
00:04:25,247 --> 00:04:29,807
The Sultan welcomed them and
refused to hand them over.

55
00:04:29,887 --> 00:04:36,367
And on top of that, he demanded tribute
from the emir of Erzincan, Timur's vassal.

56
00:04:37,217 --> 00:04:46,237
So Timur sent his envoys to Bayezid's
court, bearing a sealed letter.

57
00:04:46,317 --> 00:04:52,267
The chronicles say the words the two
men exchanged struck harder than swords.

58
00:04:53,117 --> 00:04:59,816
Timur wrote, as the chronicles paraphrase
it: Know that most of the earth,

59
00:04:59,866 --> 00:05:05,977
from sea to sea, is in our grasp,
and that kings stand at our door.

60
00:05:06,057 --> 00:05:11,207
Hand over our enemies, and do
not overstep your measure…

61
00:05:11,287 --> 00:05:14,867
for if you refuse, you will see
what you do not wish to see.

62
00:05:15,717 --> 00:05:17,867
The reply came as fire.

63
00:05:17,947 --> 00:05:22,166
Bayezid scorned Timur as a
highway robber, not a king,

64
00:05:22,216 --> 00:05:26,327
and dared him to come out
and fight if he was a man.

65
00:05:26,407 --> 00:05:33,267
The chronicles say he sealed his letter with
an insult against Timur's own household…

66
00:05:33,347 --> 00:05:37,100
an insult that, among Timur's
people, only blood could wash away.

67
00:05:37,950 --> 00:05:41,080
Timur read the letter in silence.

68
00:05:41,160 --> 00:05:43,620
Then he closed it…

69
00:05:43,700 --> 00:05:46,750
and ordered the drums to be beaten.

70
00:05:46,830 --> 00:05:52,667
In the summer of 1400,
his armies moved west.

71
00:05:56,850 --> 00:05:59,260
Sivas was the first step.

72
00:05:59,340 --> 00:06:02,507
Timur besieged it for eighteen days; his men

73
00:06:02,557 --> 00:06:05,797
tunnelled under the
walls until they cracked,

74
00:06:05,847 --> 00:06:10,600
and the city surrendered on a
promise that blood would be spared.

75
00:06:10,680 --> 00:06:13,724
But the promise did not cover the defenders:

76
00:06:13,774 --> 00:06:17,430
thousands of them were buried
alive in the trenches.

77
00:06:17,510 --> 00:06:21,662
Ottoman chronicles say that
Ertuğrul, Bayezid's son and

78
00:06:21,712 --> 00:06:26,067
governor of the city, was
killed there on Timur's orders.

79
00:06:26,883 --> 00:06:30,922
When the news reached the
Sultan, it is said he wept for

80
00:06:30,972 --> 00:06:35,353
his son as he had wept for no
one before, and swore revenge.

81
00:06:35,433 --> 00:06:38,083
But Timur did not wait for him.

82
00:06:38,163 --> 00:06:43,128
He turned south like a storm,
took Aleppo, then Damascus,

83
00:06:43,178 --> 00:06:47,313
then returned to Baghdad
and left it in rubble.

84
00:06:47,393 --> 00:06:50,833
He was securing his back
before the great battle.

85
00:06:51,683 --> 00:06:55,268
Bayezid seized on his
absence, marched on Erzincan,

86
00:06:55,318 --> 00:06:59,023
and captured the family of
its emir, Timur's vassal.

87
00:06:59,103 --> 00:07:01,643
It was a slap in the face of the Lame.

88
00:07:01,723 --> 00:07:05,389
Timur spent the winter on
the pastures of Karabakh,

89
00:07:05,439 --> 00:07:08,353
preparing the greatest army of his life.

90
00:07:08,433 --> 00:07:09,683
And when spring came…

91
00:07:09,763 --> 00:07:11,600
there was nothing left to say.

92
00:07:12,450 --> 00:07:16,198
In the spring of 1402,
Bayezid lifted the siege of

93
00:07:16,248 --> 00:07:19,843
Constantinople and gathered
his armies at Bursa:

94
00:07:19,893 --> 00:07:24,096
the Janissaries, the Sipahi
cavalry, the Tatar horsemen,

95
00:07:24,146 --> 00:07:27,286
the troops of the emirates he had annexed,

96
00:07:27,336 --> 00:07:30,830
and the Serbian knights
in their heavy armour.

97
00:07:30,910 --> 00:07:37,200
And with him, his sons:
Süleyman, İsa, Musa and Mehmed.

98
00:07:38,050 --> 00:07:40,913
The Sultan advanced east toward Tokat,

99
00:07:40,963 --> 00:07:44,950
waiting for his enemy on
ground of his own choosing.

100
00:07:45,030 --> 00:07:47,610
But Timur did not come.

101
00:07:47,690 --> 00:07:51,858
He swung south through Kayseri and suddenly

102
00:07:51,908 --> 00:07:56,910
appeared behind the Ottoman
army, besieging Ankara.

103
00:07:56,990 --> 00:08:02,070
The Thunderbolt, used to
surprising his enemies…

104
00:08:02,150 --> 00:08:04,533
was surprised this time.

105
00:08:05,383 --> 00:08:10,133
Bayezid turned back, on an exhausting
march under the July heat.

106
00:08:10,213 --> 00:08:16,433
When his soldiers reached the plain of Çubuk
near Ankara, they were burning with thirst.

107
00:08:16,513 --> 00:08:19,523
But Timur had reached the water first:

108
00:08:19,573 --> 00:08:25,933
he diverted the stream behind his camp
and spoiled the wells along their route.

109
00:08:26,013 --> 00:08:28,663
The battle had already begun…

110
00:08:28,743 --> 00:08:30,533
before a single sword was drawn.

111
00:08:31,383 --> 00:08:39,483
On the last night, the fires of both
camps burned as far as the eye could see.

112
00:08:39,563 --> 00:08:46,103
Modern estimates put Bayezid's army at around
a hundred thousand, and Timur's at more.

113
00:08:46,183 --> 00:08:51,353
And in the Ottoman ranks, the
Tatar horsemen were whispering…

114
00:08:51,433 --> 00:08:54,533
for Timur's letters had
reached them before his armies.

115
00:08:56,883 --> 00:09:05,343
At dawn on the twenty-eighth of
July, the two armies drew up.

116
00:09:05,423 --> 00:09:08,542
In the Ottoman centre stood
Bayezid with the Janissaries;

117
00:09:08,592 --> 00:09:12,099
on his right the Serbs under
Stefan; on his left Prince Süleyman

118
00:09:12,149 --> 00:09:15,373
with the Anatolian troops; and
in the rear, Mehmed Çelebi.

119
00:09:15,453 --> 00:09:19,478
Facing them, Timur's
grandson Muhammad Sultan

120
00:09:19,528 --> 00:09:23,873
led the vanguard, with
the elephants before him.

121
00:09:23,953 --> 00:09:27,623
As for the Lame, he sat on a hill…

122
00:09:27,703 --> 00:09:28,903
watching.

123
00:09:30,417 --> 00:09:33,247
The arrows fell like rain.

124
00:09:33,327 --> 00:09:38,351
Timurid horsemen struck
the Ottoman wings wave

125
00:09:38,401 --> 00:09:43,807
after wave, shooting,
withdrawing, and returning.

126
00:09:43,887 --> 00:09:49,877
The Ottomans answered bravely,
and Süleyman's wing almost held.

127
00:09:49,957 --> 00:09:54,800
But thirst and the sun were
fighting on Timur's side.

128
00:09:58,650 --> 00:10:01,390
Then what had been plotted
in the dark came to pass.

129
00:10:01,470 --> 00:10:11,240
The Tatar horsemen suddenly turned and
fell upon their own comrades in arms.

130
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:13,050
And when the riders of Aydın, Saruhan,

131
00:10:13,100 --> 00:10:15,626
Menteşe and Germiyan saw
their former princes beckoning

132
00:10:15,676 --> 00:10:17,830
to them from Timur's
ranks, they crossed over.

133
00:10:17,910 --> 00:10:24,200
Within a single hour, the
entire left wing collapsed.

134
00:10:25,050 --> 00:10:28,520
Only the right wing did not give way.

135
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:35,798
The Serbs held firm in their iron armour
and threw back attack after attack, until,

136
00:10:35,848 --> 00:10:42,310
it is said, Timur himself exclaimed:
these poor wretches fight like lions.

137
00:10:42,390 --> 00:10:49,940
Stefan cut his way to the Sultan, begging
him to withdraw while there was still time.

138
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:51,667
Bayezid refused.

139
00:10:52,517 --> 00:10:58,417
So Stefan protected Prince Süleyman
and cleared him a path to the west.

140
00:10:58,497 --> 00:11:04,371
The sons scattered: Süleyman
to Bursa, Mehmed to Amasya,

141
00:11:04,421 --> 00:11:08,547
and İsa wherever escape would take him.

142
00:11:08,627 --> 00:11:12,477
As for Musa, he was taken prisoner.

143
00:11:12,557 --> 00:11:14,477
And the father remained…

144
00:11:14,557 --> 00:11:15,757
alone.

145
00:11:16,217 --> 00:11:19,728
On a small hill, about
a thousand Janissaries

146
00:11:19,778 --> 00:11:23,657
gathered around Bayezid
and fought until evening.

147
00:11:23,737 --> 00:11:27,210
The chronicles say the Sultan swung his axe

148
00:11:27,260 --> 00:11:30,947
with his own hand as
enemies fell around him.

149
00:11:31,027 --> 00:11:35,027
When the last of his
men had fallen, he tried

150
00:11:35,077 --> 00:11:39,127
to flee on horseback,
but his horse stumbled…

151
00:11:39,207 --> 00:11:44,277
and the horsemen of Mahmud
Khan surrounded him.

152
00:11:44,357 --> 00:11:48,907
And so, after thirteen years of victories…

153
00:11:48,987 --> 00:11:51,167
the Thunderbolt fell captive.

154
00:11:55,850 --> 00:11:58,810
Bayezid was brought into Timur's tent.

155
00:11:58,890 --> 00:12:03,909
The chronicles say the Lame rose to
receive him, seated him at his side,

156
00:12:03,959 --> 00:12:09,401
then reproached him for all that had
happened: for the letters, for the pride,

157
00:12:09,451 --> 00:12:12,690
and for the blood that
could have been spared.

158
00:12:12,770 --> 00:12:16,820
And the two men sat face to
face: the master of Asia…

159
00:12:16,900 --> 00:12:19,033
and the Thunderbolt that had burned out.

160
00:12:19,850 --> 00:12:21,970
Here the accounts diverge.

161
00:12:22,050 --> 00:12:27,010
It is said that Timur asked him: what would
you have done with me had you captured me?

162
00:12:27,090 --> 00:12:31,580
Bayezid replied: I would have
locked you in an iron cage.

163
00:12:31,660 --> 00:12:34,900
And Timur said: then that is your reward.

164
00:12:34,980 --> 00:12:39,612
Other historians believe he was
carried in a latticed litter,

165
00:12:39,662 --> 00:12:44,190
and that the cage is a legend
born of a misunderstood word.

166
00:12:44,270 --> 00:12:47,241
What is certain is that Timur's armies swept

167
00:12:47,291 --> 00:12:50,380
across Anatolia as far
as Bursa, his capital…

168
00:12:50,460 --> 00:12:52,900
while he was a prisoner, powerless.

169
00:12:53,750 --> 00:12:57,020
The Thunderbolt's heart could
not bear the long captivity.

170
00:12:57,100 --> 00:13:02,051
On the eighth of March,
1403, Bayezid died at

171
00:13:02,101 --> 00:13:06,880
Akşehir, at about forty-three years of age.

172
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:11,430
Some say of illness, some of grief,
and some claim he poisoned himself.

173
00:13:11,510 --> 00:13:16,260
Timur released his son Musa to
carry his father's body to Bursa…

174
00:13:16,340 --> 00:13:17,967
where he rests to this day.

175
00:13:20,317 --> 00:13:26,227
But the cruellest legacy of Ankara was
not the defeat, but what came after it.

176
00:13:26,307 --> 00:13:30,749
The state was divided among
the brothers: Süleyman in

177
00:13:30,799 --> 00:13:35,377
Edirne, İsa in Bursa, Mehmed
in Amasya, and then Musa.

178
00:13:35,457 --> 00:13:40,947
Eleven years of war between brothers,
which historians call the Interregnum.

179
00:13:41,027 --> 00:13:44,817
İsa fell, then Süleyman, then Musa…

180
00:13:44,897 --> 00:13:51,600
until only Mehmed remained, and
he reunited the state in 1413.

181
00:13:52,450 --> 00:13:55,430
As for Timur, he did not
outlive him for long.

182
00:13:55,510 --> 00:14:02,510
In the winter of 1405 he set out to conquer
China, and died on the way, at Otrar.

183
00:14:02,590 --> 00:14:09,040
His empire was torn apart among his sons
and grandsons, just as Bayezid's had been.

184
00:14:09,120 --> 00:14:12,645
And half a century after
Ankara, Mehmed's grandson,

185
00:14:12,695 --> 00:14:15,850
Mehmed the Conqueror,
entered Constantinople…

186
00:14:15,930 --> 00:14:19,633
the city that battle had
spared for fifty years.

187
00:14:20,483 --> 00:14:24,443
The two men met because the earth
was too small for two kings.

188
00:14:24,523 --> 00:14:27,953
In the end, all that remained of them were

189
00:14:28,003 --> 00:14:31,813
two tombs: one in Bursa,
and one in Samarkand.

190
00:14:31,893 --> 00:14:36,886
And between them, an old lesson the
days repeat to every generation:

191
00:14:36,936 --> 00:14:40,273
a thunderbolt lights up
the sky for a moment…

192
00:14:40,353 --> 00:14:42,967
but it never makes the dawn.

193
00:14:44,433 --> 00:14:51,433
THE THUNDERBOLT AND THE LAME
